{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-21465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x)=-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}+6,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-21465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}=-4{x}^{2}(x-\\frac{3}{2}).\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-4{x}^{2}(x-\\frac{3}{2})=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-4(-1)^{2}(-1-\\frac{3}{2})=10>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-4(1)^{2}(1-\\frac{3}{2})=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-4(2)^{2}(2-\\frac{3}{2})=-8<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-21465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "29ffdc5fe701c7fbb27fccb38a84e7f36a10572d38babf0cb732342ed8ea75ef"} {"question" "q-21465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-21465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-21465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-22754" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-2x+2},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum\n- $x=1$ is a local minimum\n- $x=2$ is a local maximum" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-22754" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2x+2)-(x-1)\\cdot (2x-2)}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+2-2{x}^{2}+4x-2}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+2x}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{2}+2x}{({x}^{2}-2x+2)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(2-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ and $x=2$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{-(-1)^{2}+2(-1)}{((-1)^{2}-2(-1)+2)^{2}}=-\\frac{3}{25}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{-(1)^{2}+2(1)}{((1)^{2}-2(1)+2)^{2}}=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{-(3)^{2}+2(3)}{((3)^{2}-2(3)+2)^{2}}=-\\frac{3}{25}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in the table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-22754" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "29ffdc5fe701c7fbb27fccb38a84e7f36a10572d38babf0cb732342ed8ea75ef"} {"question" "q-22754" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-22754" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-22754" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-113763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+3x-2,$ which of the following statements are true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-113763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+6x+3 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+2x+1) \\\\  & =3(x+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3(x+1)^{2}=0\\,⇒\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nSo $x=-1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =3(-2+1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3(0+1)^{2}=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=-1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=-1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-113763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "29ffdc5fe701c7fbb27fccb38a84e7f36a10572d38babf0cb732342ed8ea75ef"} {"question" "q-113763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-113763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema"] ["text" "There is a local maximum at $x=-1$"] ["text" "There is a local maximum at $x=1$"] ["text" "There is a local minimum at $x=-1$"] ["text" "There is a local minimum at $x=1$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-113763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-113765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x)=7-{x}^{7},$ which of the following statements are true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-113765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-7{x}^{6}.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-7{x}^{6}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-7(-1)^{6}=-7<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-7(1)^{6}=-7<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-113765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "29ffdc5fe701c7fbb27fccb38a84e7f36a10572d38babf0cb732342ed8ea75ef"} {"question" "q-113765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-113765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema"] ["text" "There is a local maximum at $x=0$"] ["text" "There is a local maximum at $x=7$"] ["text" "There is a local minimum at $x=0$"] ["text" "There is a local minimum at $x=7$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-113765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-314706" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}{e}^{x}$ have a local minimum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local minimum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-314706" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(x-1)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x}+2(x-1){e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(x-1)(x-1+2) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(x-1)(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}(x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & ={e}^{-2}(-3)(-1)=3{e}^{-2}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & ={e}^{0}(-1)(1)=-1<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & ={e}^{2}(1)(3)=3{e}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-1$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039809/state.json" "file_sha256" "9729255cdb6e0d5baa1e65817e02d16bda43fdc8f2ff91b7efaecc317bfca28e"} {"question" "q-3